1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,696
The third of February, 2023.

2
00:00:03,057 --> 00:00:04,796
8:13 in the evening.

3
00:00:04,998 --> 00:00:06,308
Salem, Ohio.

4
00:00:06,827 --> 00:00:11,245
A freight train is passing a factory,
and a surveillance camera records a fire

5
00:00:11,285 --> 00:00:12,690
under one of its cars.

6
00:00:12,883 --> 00:00:15,523
The 23rd car, a covered hopper.

7
00:00:15,843 --> 00:00:19,494
The fire is at a wheel, at the
bearing on its leading axle.

8
00:00:19,814 --> 00:00:24,371
Moments later the train crosses a hot
bearing detector, an infrared sensor

9
00:00:24,411 --> 00:00:25,534
beside the track.

10
00:00:25,739 --> 00:00:30,482
It reads that bearing at 103 degrees
Fahrenheit above the air temperature.

11
00:00:30,595 --> 00:00:35,262
The line at which the crew would be
told to slow down and stop is 170.

12
00:00:35,496 --> 00:00:37,429
So the detector sends an alert.

13
00:00:37,566 --> 00:00:40,014
Not to the train, but to a help desk.

14
00:00:40,184 --> 00:00:41,842
In the cab, nothing.

15
00:00:42,076 --> 00:00:44,383
The train is Norfolk Southern 32N.

16
00:00:44,423 --> 00:00:49,859
149 cars, 9,309 feet long.

17
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:54,864
Why that line sat at 170 is on the
record, in the railroad industry's own

18
00:00:54,904 --> 00:00:56,830
words, at a federal hearing.

19
00:00:56,922 --> 00:00:58,752
We will hear them later in this film.

20
00:00:59,070 --> 00:01:04,463
39 minutes after Salem, the train crosses
the next detector, in East Palestine.

21
00:01:04,626 --> 00:01:08,625
This time the reading is
253 degrees above the air.

22
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:11,828
That is the highest number
this kind of detector can show.

23
00:01:12,006 --> 00:01:13,955
The real temperature may have been higher.

24
00:01:14,199 --> 00:01:16,473
In the cab, the speakers announce it.

25
00:01:16,523 --> 00:01:20,270
Critical alarm, critical
alarm, critical alarm.

26
00:01:20,458 --> 00:01:22,772
The engineer begins to slow the train.

27
00:01:22,963 --> 00:01:27,994
The NTSB, America's accident investigator,
would find that the crew handled

28
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:31,400
the train properly and responded
to the alarm as they should.

29
00:01:31,554 --> 00:01:34,955
Less than a minute after the
alarm, the axle came apart.

30
00:01:35,094 --> 00:01:38,546
38 cars left the track, and
the wreckage caught fire.

31
00:01:38,684 --> 00:01:41,764
About 2,000 people were
told to leave their homes.

32
00:01:41,932 --> 00:01:45,667
This film follows the warning system
and the decisions that followed.

33
00:01:45,733 --> 00:01:48,892
The health and environmental
effects are a separate story.

34
00:01:49,129 --> 00:01:53,434
Three days later, a second number would
decide what happened to five tank cars

35
00:01:53,474 --> 00:01:54,815
of vinyl chloride.

36
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:58,832
The investigators could not find it in
the written guidance used at the scene.

37
00:01:58,929 --> 00:02:02,843
Two numbers: one on the first
night, one on the fourth day.

38
00:02:03,037 --> 00:02:05,375
So where did the margin of error run out?

39
00:02:08,915 --> 00:02:13,690
Each end of each axle on a railcar turns
inside a roller bearing, sealed and

40
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:14,993
packed with grease.

41
00:02:15,089 --> 00:02:18,487
When a bearing starts to fail,
friction turns into heat.

42
00:02:18,610 --> 00:02:22,493
So railroads line their tracks with
detectors that look up at each bearing as

43
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:25,795
it passes, and measure how
much hotter it is than the air.

44
00:02:26,344 --> 00:02:30,385
On Norfolk Southern, three lines
decided what happened next.

45
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,193
90 degrees above the air: an alert
to the help desk, which watches the

46
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:36,295
next readings.

47
00:02:36,564 --> 00:02:42,803
170: a message to the crew, who must
slow down, and then stop to inspect.

48
00:02:42,924 --> 00:02:46,911
And a critical alarm, for which
the crew must stop at once.

49
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:52,251
Before this accident, Norfolk Southern
set that critical line at 200 degrees.

50
00:02:52,461 --> 00:02:55,538
At Salem the reading was 103.

51
00:02:55,844 --> 00:02:57,294
Above the first line.

52
00:02:57,413 --> 00:02:58,681
Below the second.

53
00:02:58,762 --> 00:03:00,675
But the camera showed fire.

54
00:03:00,835 --> 00:03:02,760
How could the bearing read so low?

55
00:03:03,063 --> 00:03:07,436
The detector is a pair of infrared
sensors looking up from the track, and at

56
00:03:07,476 --> 00:03:10,785
Salem they read the inner edge
of the bearing's outer cup.

57
00:03:10,952 --> 00:03:15,342
A failing bearing heats from the
inside, and the outside lags behind.

58
00:03:15,489 --> 00:03:19,975
The NTSB found that the Salem
reading did not reflect the bearing's

59
00:03:20,015 --> 00:03:21,156
true temperature.

60
00:03:21,257 --> 00:03:25,601
And once a bearing is failing, the report
notes, it can go from below an alarm

61
00:03:25,641 --> 00:03:28,623
temperature to seized
in less than a minute.

62
00:03:28,766 --> 00:03:31,983
The analyst on the help desk
did not see the Salem alert.

63
00:03:32,023 --> 00:03:34,999
He was working on other,
higher-priority alerts.

64
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:38,086
The investigators found it would
not have changed the outcome:

65
00:03:38,127 --> 00:03:41,594
the train derailed before the
procedures required him to act.

66
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:45,449
If you have read about this derailment,
you may have heard a simple answer:

67
00:03:45,590 --> 00:03:48,497
a skipped repair, a
bearing with no grease.

68
00:03:48,685 --> 00:03:49,665
Hold that thought.

69
00:03:49,705 --> 00:03:51,410
The record can't confirm it.

70
00:03:51,493 --> 00:03:55,708
What it does show is stranger:
where the warning line came from.

71
00:03:55,861 --> 00:03:58,526
The detectors themselves were miles apart.

72
00:03:58,593 --> 00:04:02,271
From Salem to East
Palestine, about 19 miles.

73
00:04:02,335 --> 00:04:07,478
Norfolk Southern testified that its
average spacing on key routes was 13.9.

74
00:04:07,541 --> 00:04:11,893
The spacing met the industry's
recommended practice, 40 miles or less.

75
00:04:12,109 --> 00:04:16,760
At the detector before Salem, in
Sebring, about 11 miles back, the same

76
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:20,347
bearing read 38 degrees,
below the alert line.

77
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:23,858
From there to a broken
axle: about 30 miles.

78
00:04:24,059 --> 00:04:28,964
Both can be true at once: a slow build
over many miles, then a failure in

79
00:04:29,004 --> 00:04:30,004
under a minute.

80
00:04:30,098 --> 00:04:34,508
At Salem the reading was over the help
desk line and under the crew line.

81
00:04:34,615 --> 00:04:37,793
Had the crew line been lower,
the crew would have been told.

82
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:42,503
Whether that would have stopped this
train in time, the NTSB did not decide:

83
00:04:42,543 --> 00:04:45,986
it found that research is needed to
tell whether lower alert and alarm

84
00:04:46,026 --> 00:04:49,008
thresholds would make a
significant safety improvement.

85
00:04:49,126 --> 00:04:52,571
Either way: where did 170 come from?

86
00:04:55,865 --> 00:04:58,495
The answer is in the industry's rulebook.

87
00:04:58,709 --> 00:05:04,163
The AAR, the Association of American
Railroads, defines an overheated bearing

88
00:05:04,203 --> 00:05:08,033
as one at least 170 degrees above the air.

89
00:05:08,275 --> 00:05:13,187
The NTSB found that this definition is
based on what makes a repair billable

90
00:05:13,227 --> 00:05:14,690
to the railcar's owner.

91
00:05:14,821 --> 00:05:19,948
It relates, the board wrote, to billing
for repairs, not directly to safety.

92
00:05:20,281 --> 00:05:25,189
In June 2023, at a public hearing in
East Palestine, the board's technical

93
00:05:25,229 --> 00:05:30,196
panel put that to the AAR's senior vice
president for safety and operations.

94
00:05:30,613 --> 00:05:31,793
[AAR witness] Now, going to the issue
of how we arrived at those thresholds.

95
00:05:31,833 --> 00:05:34,133
So let's talk, first of all,
what those thresholds represent.

96
00:05:34,173 --> 00:05:37,233
Those are thresholds in AAR's car repair

97
00:05:37,273 --> 00:05:43,333
billing rules which authorize the ability

98
00:05:43,713 --> 00:05:49,673
of a railroad to take a car out of
service, make the repairs that are

99
00:05:49,713 --> 00:05:54,813
necessary. Those threshold
— the way in which the

100
00:05:54,853 --> 00:05:59,813
thresholds are developed really is
a function of experience, and you're

101
00:05:59,853 --> 00:06:05,403
balancing a couple of considerations.
One of those considerations is,

102
00:06:05,443 --> 00:06:11,863
of course, the detection of
issues with the bearings.

103
00:06:12,123 --> 00:06:16,783
The other issue, as it was talked about
earlier, is — you know, if you put the

104
00:06:16,823 --> 00:06:21,003
thresholds too low, you're going
to have a lot of false stops

105
00:06:21,605 --> 00:06:26,369
Then a member of the NTSB's technical
panel asked whether these thresholds are

106
00:06:26,409 --> 00:06:28,571
limits for safe operation at all.

107
00:06:29,863 --> 00:06:34,313
[NTSB panel] Thank you. So these
overheated bearing temperature

108
00:06:34,353 --> 00:06:37,373
thresholds established in the rule,

109
00:06:37,413 --> 00:06:44,193
then, are not absolute limits for safe
operating temperatures of bearings?

110
00:06:45,073 --> 00:06:50,173
If I understand properly what you just
described, they are a standard at which

111
00:06:50,213 --> 00:06:53,273
bearings can be removed from service and

112
00:06:53,313 --> 00:06:56,313
billing and the car owners
could be billed for it?

113
00:06:56,933 --> 00:07:01,153
[AAR witness] Correct. Now, keep
in mind it's in the interest of

114
00:07:01,193 --> 00:07:06,173
the operating railroad not to have
derailments attributable to hot bearings

115
00:07:06,726 --> 00:07:08,151
A billing standard.

116
00:07:08,448 --> 00:07:11,418
And, in the same breath,
the industry's point:

117
00:07:11,641 --> 00:07:13,708
no railroad wants a derailment.

118
00:07:13,882 --> 00:07:18,064
The witness went on to explain that the
association can set standards only for

119
00:07:18,104 --> 00:07:22,947
cars passed between railroads, not for
what a railroad does on its own track.

120
00:07:23,128 --> 00:07:27,460
Norfolk Southern testified that it based
its own thresholds on its experience

121
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:29,324
and on the industry's standards.

122
00:07:29,437 --> 00:07:33,015
Its critical alarm, before
the accident, was 200.

123
00:07:33,209 --> 00:07:35,295
After it, 170.

124
00:07:35,502 --> 00:07:39,252
At the same hearing, the industry's
witness said the railroads were now all

125
00:07:39,292 --> 00:07:41,657
committed to using 170.

126
00:07:41,916 --> 00:07:45,984
So after East Palestine, on this
railroad, the number in the billing

127
00:07:46,024 --> 00:07:50,112
definition became the line at
which a train must stop at once.

128
00:07:50,339 --> 00:07:52,514
Every line like this is a trade.

129
00:07:52,653 --> 00:07:55,452
Set it too low, and
trains stop for nothing.

130
00:07:55,545 --> 00:07:59,003
Set it too high, and a
failing bearing keeps rolling.

131
00:07:59,092 --> 00:08:03,782
In the NTSB's words, railroads choose
their thresholds by balancing between

132
00:08:03,822 --> 00:08:07,709
preventing bearing failures
and avoiding unnecessary stops.

133
00:08:07,941 --> 00:08:10,972
No investigator links what
follows to this bearing.

134
00:08:11,069 --> 00:08:13,522
But it is how the railroad was being run.

135
00:08:13,776 --> 00:08:18,163
In February 2019, Norfolk
Southern told investors its plan.

136
00:08:18,305 --> 00:08:20,454
Precision scheduled railroading.

137
00:08:20,574 --> 00:08:24,663
An operating ratio, the share of
revenue spent running the railroad, of

138
00:08:24,703 --> 00:08:29,011
60 percent by 2021, down from 65.4.

139
00:08:29,169 --> 00:08:33,626
Continued share buybacks, using free
cash flow and borrowing capacity.

140
00:08:33,819 --> 00:08:37,164
Its five principles
included working safely.

141
00:08:37,377 --> 00:08:39,332
The filings show what followed.

142
00:08:39,467 --> 00:08:45,996
Average employees fell from 26,662
in 2018, the year before the plan,

143
00:08:46,036 --> 00:08:51,964
to 18,900 in 2022: by our
calculation, 29 percent.

144
00:08:52,119 --> 00:08:58,530
From 2019 to 2022, the company spent
$10 billion buying back its own shares.

145
00:08:59,032 --> 00:09:01,009
Did that cause this derailment?

146
00:09:01,591 --> 00:09:03,457
Here is where the record stops.

147
00:09:03,796 --> 00:09:06,877
The 23rd car was not Norfolk Southern's.

148
00:09:07,025 --> 00:09:11,497
It belonged to GATX, and was
leased to Braskem America.

149
00:09:11,828 --> 00:09:15,898
It joined the train at a yard in
Illinois run by a terminal railroad,

150
00:09:15,938 --> 00:09:20,067
part-owned by several large railroads,
Norfolk Southern among them.

151
00:09:20,306 --> 00:09:22,607
That railroad's inspectors checked it.

152
00:09:22,730 --> 00:09:25,162
Norfolk Southern's inspectors did not.

153
00:09:25,318 --> 00:09:29,529
Whether that inspection missed anything,
the investigators could not determine.

154
00:09:29,755 --> 00:09:34,484
After the derailment, the NTSB checked
the cars that stayed on the rails, and

155
00:09:34,524 --> 00:09:38,611
found reportable defects on about a
quarter of them, most of them minor.

156
00:09:38,792 --> 00:09:42,422
It obtained a Norfolk Southern
foreman's email that appeared to set a

157
00:09:42,462 --> 00:09:47,355
90-second target for each car's inspection
in Norfolk Southern's own yards.

158
00:09:47,467 --> 00:09:50,161
Car 23 was inspected elsewhere.

159
00:09:50,308 --> 00:09:53,883
And it could not determine whether
inspection time goals contributed to the

160
00:09:53,923 --> 00:09:55,809
defects that went unreported.

161
00:09:56,010 --> 00:10:01,649
In March 2023, the NTSB opened a special
investigation into Norfolk Southern's

162
00:10:01,689 --> 00:10:02,772
safety culture.

163
00:10:02,980 --> 00:10:05,088
That is our reading of the record:

164
00:10:05,229 --> 00:10:08,120
no investigator links
the plan to this bearing.

165
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:11,746
What the record does show is
where the stop line was drawn:

166
00:10:11,827 --> 00:10:15,614
at a number the industry wrote for
billing, which Norfolk Southern says it

167
00:10:15,654 --> 00:10:17,718
combined with its own experience.

168
00:10:17,861 --> 00:10:20,628
A claim you will find in
many comment sections:

169
00:10:20,729 --> 00:10:22,333
cost cutting caused this.

170
00:10:22,552 --> 00:10:24,105
The record can't say.

171
00:10:27,850 --> 00:10:28,974
Back to the bearing.

172
00:10:29,144 --> 00:10:32,753
As it fails, the rollers
and the cup grind and heat.

173
00:10:32,848 --> 00:10:34,111
The grease burns.

174
00:10:34,151 --> 00:10:35,182
The seals go.

175
00:10:35,470 --> 00:10:40,350
Then the end of the axle, the journal,
breaks away, and the wheelset drops.

176
00:10:40,525 --> 00:10:42,538
Watch the last 19 miles.

177
00:10:42,796 --> 00:10:47,919
From Salem, the train runs east,
at between 19 and 45 miles an hour.

178
00:10:48,016 --> 00:10:51,001
Between the detectors,
nothing measures the bearing.

179
00:10:51,098 --> 00:10:54,146
19 miles of track, and nothing reads it.

180
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:59,731
Milepost 49.81.

181
00:10:59,791 --> 00:11:03,120
253 degrees, the top of the scale.

182
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:04,408
Critical alarm.

183
00:11:04,513 --> 00:11:06,614
The crew begins to slow the train.

184
00:11:06,745 --> 00:11:10,399
Less than a minute later, the axle
separates, and the brakes go into

185
00:11:10,439 --> 00:11:11,904
emergency on their own.

186
00:11:12,063 --> 00:11:18,179
From the emergency, the lead locomotives
travel another 1,160 feet, and stop

187
00:11:18,219 --> 00:11:19,682
38 seconds later.

188
00:11:21,652 --> 00:11:23,021
So was it the grease?

189
00:11:23,327 --> 00:11:24,745
The record can't say.

190
00:11:25,025 --> 00:11:28,540
The investigators found that the
root cause of the bearing's failure

191
00:11:28,580 --> 00:11:29,884
remains unclear.

192
00:11:30,186 --> 00:11:35,825
Whatever started it, fatigue, a loss of
lubrication, water, was erased by the

193
00:11:35,865 --> 00:11:37,530
heat of its own failure.

194
00:11:37,726 --> 00:11:40,728
And the NTSB noted that this is a pattern:

195
00:11:40,948 --> 00:11:45,311
a bearing that fails badly enough to
derail a train tends to destroy the

196
00:11:45,351 --> 00:11:46,612
evidence of why.

197
00:11:47,009 --> 00:11:51,718
Another claim: the burn, three days
later, destroyed that evidence.

198
00:11:51,859 --> 00:11:53,629
That is not in the record.

199
00:11:53,807 --> 00:11:58,082
Investigators recovered the bearing's
pieces on the fifth of February, west of

200
00:11:58,122 --> 00:12:00,910
the wreck, where they had
fallen in the first moments.

201
00:12:01,047 --> 00:12:04,482
The burn was on the
sixth, on five other cars.

202
00:12:04,730 --> 00:12:08,980
The fire, the NTSB found,
likely began with a tank car.

203
00:12:09,168 --> 00:12:14,172
In the derailment, a DOT-111 tank
car carrying a flammable liquid was

204
00:12:14,212 --> 00:12:16,894
punctured, and its contents fed the fire.

205
00:12:17,042 --> 00:12:21,205
In the same pile lay five pressure
tank cars of vinyl chloride.

206
00:12:21,245 --> 00:12:25,270
Heat from the fire set off the relief
valves on four of them, the valves that

207
00:12:25,310 --> 00:12:28,253
open when the pressure
inside a tank gets too high.

208
00:12:28,333 --> 00:12:31,262
The responders did not yet
know what was on the train.

209
00:12:31,413 --> 00:12:36,103
The NTSB found that Norfolk Southern's
delay in sending the list of cars kept

210
00:12:36,143 --> 00:12:39,890
responders near the fire longer than
they needed to be, and delayed the

211
00:12:39,930 --> 00:12:41,232
evacuation order.

212
00:12:41,407 --> 00:12:45,763
Vinyl chloride is the raw
material of PVC, a plastic.

213
00:12:45,812 --> 00:12:50,410
Its molecules can join into long chains,
and that reaction gives off heat.

214
00:12:50,530 --> 00:12:55,428
In a closed tank, a runaway reaction
could build pressure until the tank fails.

215
00:12:55,543 --> 00:12:56,656
That was the fear.

216
00:12:56,922 --> 00:12:58,228
Now the second number.

217
00:12:58,353 --> 00:13:02,262
On the evening of the fourth of February,
the shipper of the vinyl chloride,

218
00:13:02,302 --> 00:13:07,172
Oxy Vinyls, told Norfolk Southern and
its contractors it saw a low probability

219
00:13:07,212 --> 00:13:08,354
of that reaction.

220
00:13:08,533 --> 00:13:12,547
The hottest car's temperature, measured
by those contractors, peaked at about

221
00:13:12,587 --> 00:13:14,975
138 degrees Fahrenheit.

222
00:13:15,121 --> 00:13:18,966
This time that is the actual
temperature, not a number above the air.

223
00:13:19,142 --> 00:13:23,437
By the afternoon of the sixth,
it had fallen to about 126.

224
00:13:23,683 --> 00:13:27,511
That afternoon the incident commander,
the village's fire chief, met the

225
00:13:27,551 --> 00:13:31,478
governors of Ohio and Pennsylvania
and dozens of other officials.

226
00:13:31,606 --> 00:13:35,924
Oxy Vinyls was not invited, the
incident commander told investigators.

227
00:13:36,055 --> 00:13:40,445
The NTSB found no evidence that anyone
outside Norfolk Southern and its

228
00:13:40,485 --> 00:13:43,010
contractors knew the shipper disagreed.

229
00:13:43,475 --> 00:13:45,271
Then came a smaller meeting.

230
00:13:45,623 --> 00:13:52,135
Two contractor firms told him that
if a car reached 153 to 158 degrees,

231
00:13:52,275 --> 00:13:54,479
the reaction would run out of control.

232
00:13:54,918 --> 00:13:58,943
The investigators did not find that range
in the written guidance consulted at

233
00:13:58,983 --> 00:14:01,485
the scene, or in the shipper's messages.

234
00:14:01,898 --> 00:14:05,877
The incident commander said he
was given 13 minutes to decide.

235
00:14:06,123 --> 00:14:10,729
The burn, they told him, had to start
before three in the afternoon, to finish

236
00:14:10,769 --> 00:14:15,054
in daylight and avoid a temperature
inversion, a layer of air that would keep

237
00:14:15,094 --> 00:14:17,016
the vapor cloud from dispersing.

238
00:14:17,312 --> 00:14:19,384
The incident commander consented.

239
00:14:19,637 --> 00:14:22,362
The charges went off at 4:37.

240
00:14:26,779 --> 00:14:31,331
Three in the afternoon, the NTSB
noted, was not a hard deadline.

241
00:14:31,602 --> 00:14:34,059
Two numbers decided this town's week.

242
00:14:34,349 --> 00:14:36,785
One matched a definition
written for billing.

243
00:14:36,887 --> 00:14:41,067
The other, investigators could not
find in the guidance used at the scene.

244
00:14:42,838 --> 00:14:48,015
The NTSB's finding: the temperatures on
scene did not show that reaction, and

245
00:14:48,055 --> 00:14:50,462
examinations afterwards confirmed it.

246
00:14:50,665 --> 00:14:53,296
The vent and burn was not necessary.

247
00:14:53,696 --> 00:14:57,566
Norfolk Southern and its contractors,
the board found, compromised the

248
00:14:57,606 --> 00:15:01,456
integrity of the decision by
creating unwarranted urgency.

249
00:15:01,791 --> 00:15:06,257
The board also found that one industry
guide consulted at the scene overstated

250
00:15:06,297 --> 00:15:07,879
the risk of that reaction.

251
00:15:08,259 --> 00:15:12,203
Norfolk Southern's answer: the only
motivation of the company and its

252
00:15:12,243 --> 00:15:15,747
contractors was the health and
safety of the community and the first

253
00:15:15,787 --> 00:15:20,032
responders, and the incident command had
been told about 20 hours ahead that a

254
00:15:20,072 --> 00:15:21,373
burn might be needed.

255
00:15:21,606 --> 00:15:24,998
The company does not agree that
it withheld the shipper's views.

256
00:15:25,211 --> 00:15:29,508
The incident commander told investigators
that he bore the responsibility.

257
00:15:29,884 --> 00:15:31,750
One more claim you will often see:

258
00:15:31,992 --> 00:15:34,561
they burned it because it
was the cheapest option.

259
00:15:34,830 --> 00:15:36,641
That is not in the record.

260
00:15:36,886 --> 00:15:41,156
What is in the record is the urgency,
the information the incident commander

261
00:15:41,196 --> 00:15:44,303
was given, and the reason
Norfolk Southern gives.

262
00:15:44,493 --> 00:15:48,391
The NTSB itself could not determine
the main reason for the company's

263
00:15:48,431 --> 00:15:49,796
preference for the burn.

264
00:15:53,555 --> 00:15:58,056
In June 2024, the NTSB
adopted its final report.

265
00:15:58,310 --> 00:16:02,814
The probable cause: the failure of
the bearing on the 23rd car, which

266
00:16:02,854 --> 00:16:05,696
overheated and caused
the axle to separate.

267
00:16:06,118 --> 00:16:10,682
Norfolk Southern's annual reports put
its net cost from the derailment, after

268
00:16:10,722 --> 00:16:15,526
insurance, at about
$1.1 billion in 2023, and

269
00:16:15,566 --> 00:16:18,668
$325 million in 2024.

270
00:16:19,233 --> 00:16:24,206
In 2025, insurance and other
recoveries were larger than new costs,

271
00:16:24,246 --> 00:16:26,471
by $254 million.

272
00:16:26,935 --> 00:16:31,365
A class action settlement created
a $600 million fund for residents

273
00:16:31,405 --> 00:16:32,405
and businesses.

274
00:16:32,808 --> 00:16:35,508
The appeals against the
settlement were dismissed.

275
00:16:36,114 --> 00:16:40,100
An appeal over residents' personal
injury releases was still pending

276
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:41,963
in October 2026.

277
00:16:42,611 --> 00:16:45,957
A separate settlement with the federal
government still awaited the court's

278
00:16:45,997 --> 00:16:47,479
approval at that date.

279
00:16:47,984 --> 00:16:53,277
In March 2026, letting residents join
that case, the judge's order repeated

280
00:16:53,317 --> 00:16:57,887
words from an earlier hearing:
this is not a rubber stamp court.

281
00:16:58,266 --> 00:16:59,492
Could it happen again?

282
00:17:00,066 --> 00:17:05,837
As of July 2026, federal rules did not
require trackside detectors at all, or

283
00:17:05,877 --> 00:17:08,481
set the temperature at
which a car must be stopped.

284
00:17:08,886 --> 00:17:14,195
The NTSB has recommended that the FRA,
the federal railroad regulator, set

285
00:17:14,235 --> 00:17:18,001
minimum requirements, including
alarm thresholds and the distance

286
00:17:18,041 --> 00:17:19,283
between detectors.

287
00:17:19,604 --> 00:17:25,530
Norfolk Southern said in 2024 that it
was installing 187 more detectors, to

288
00:17:25,570 --> 00:17:29,374
bring the average spacing on its
core network to about 12 miles.

289
00:17:29,879 --> 00:17:34,263
And the kind of tank car the investigators
found likely started the fire stays

290
00:17:34,303 --> 00:17:38,307
legal in flammable service
until the first of May, 2029.

291
00:17:38,734 --> 00:17:43,461
Retiring them sooner, the investigators
found, is feasible, but unlikely,

292
00:17:43,522 --> 00:17:46,286
because of economic and
business disincentives.

293
00:17:46,652 --> 00:17:48,062
Two last claims.

294
00:17:48,404 --> 00:17:50,892
The brake rule repealed in 2018:

295
00:17:51,174 --> 00:17:55,407
it applied to a different kind of
train, and this train did not qualify.

296
00:17:55,770 --> 00:17:59,090
Too few crew: there were
three people in the cab.

297
00:17:59,474 --> 00:18:05,147
As of July 2026, no federal rule sets the
temperature at which a train must stop.

298
00:18:05,364 --> 00:18:07,550
The NTSB has asked for one.

299
00:18:07,924 --> 00:18:10,652
From below the alert
line to a broken axle:

300
00:18:10,774 --> 00:18:12,178
about 30 miles.

301
00:18:12,314 --> 00:18:15,316
The last two detectors
were 19 miles apart.
